by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-04T10:04:42Z

Both applied @stof, thanks.

```php
 * @param \Closure(string): mixed                                                                               $callback
 * @param int-mask<\FILTER_REQUIRE_SCALAR, \FILTER_REQUIRE_ARRAY, \FILTER_FORCE_ARRAY, \FILTER_NULL_ON_FAILURE> $flags
```

Your caveat was worth raising, and the answer is that neither the wildcard nor the union works here.

The wildcard form is class-constant only, so `int-mask-of<FILTER_*>` and `int-mask-of<\FILTER_*>` are both parse errors, in PHPStan (`Unexpected token "*", expected '>'`) and in Psalm (`Invalid type 'FILTER_*'`). Every existing use in the repo is `self::PARSE_*` or `Yaml::DUMP_*`, which is why they work. And the constants would have been wrong anyway: `FILTER_*` covers the filter ids too, `FILTER_CALLBACK` at 1024 and `FILTER_SANITIZE_*` at 513 to 523, not just the flags.

Spelling the union out as `int-mask-of<A|B|C>` parses in PHPStan but Psalm rejects it with `Union types are not allowed in value-of type`. `int-mask<A, B, C>` is understood by both, so that is what is in there.

It enforces what you would want: `flags: \FILTER_CALLBACK` and `flags: \FILTER_SANITIZE_EMAIL` are both reported, while `\FILTER_REQUIRE_ARRAY | \FILTER_NULL_ON_FAILURE` passes. `FILTER_THROW_ON_FAILURE` is left out on purpose, `filter()` sets `FILTER_NULL_ON_FAILURE` itself and turns the failure into an exception, so the two would fight.
